{"agentDecisions":{"entries":[{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Reviewer dispositions are `resolved`, `unsupported`,\n  `unaffected`, `present` and `present-low`; there is no `fixed`. What is a\n  \"fixed disposition\"?\n- **Answer:** The implementer's claim. `gate.execute` and `review.execute`\n  take `claims: [{findingId, claim}]`, which PROTOCOL.md describes as\n  \"asserted fixes\" and also uses for rebuttals. Each claim gains a required\n  `disposition`: `fixed` (the implementer changed code to fix the finding) or\n  `disputed` (a rebuttal: the finding is not a defect or is intentionally\n  unchanged). A `fixed` claim requires a `sweep` record; a `disputed` claim\n  must not carry one.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** (a) a reviewer disposition `fixed` (rejected: reviewers\n  verify fixes, the implementer claims them); (b) `disposition` optional, with\n  an unlabeled claim treated as not fixed (rejected: an implementer could skip\n  the sweep by leaving the label off, which defeats AC4); (c) every claim is a\n  fix (rejected: rebuttals are claims too).\n- **Reversibility:** an input-contract change; callers add one field. A later\n  version can add more disposition values (for example for #148).","file":"interview.md","heading":"D1. Where a `fixed` disposition lives"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** What exactly does GateReeve check?\n- **Answer:** `sweep: {class, search, sites: [{path, line}], fix, test,\n  dependents}`. Every field is required. `class`, `search`, `fix`, `test` and\n  `dependents` are non-empty text of at most 4096 characters; `sites` has 1 to\n  256 entries, each a non-empty `path` (at most 1024 characters) and a\n  positive integer `line`. Unknown keys are rejected. A failure refuses the\n  execution before any reviewer starts and names the field, for example\n  `claims[0].sweep.search`. GateReeve checks presence and shape only; the\n  reviewers verify truth (D12 item 3). Site paths are not checked against the\n  candidate: a swept site may have been deleted by the fix.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** verify paths exist in the candidate (rejected: deleted\n  sites are legitimate, and truth is the reviewers' job); `search` inside each\n  site (rejected: the source plan has one `search` for the sweep).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible; bounds can be relaxed.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D2. Sweep record shape and validation"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** `class` is text. How does GateReeve match a later finding to\n  a swept class?\n- **Answer:** By the claimed finding's `classId`, taken from the retained\n  review that reported it (claims naming no retained finding are already\n  dropped, #141). The record keeps the implementer's `class` text alongside.\n  Reviewers are told to give a missed site of a swept class that record's\n  `classId`.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** the implementer supplies a `classId` (rejected: it would\n  let the implementer relabel the class reviewers named); match by class text\n  (rejected: free text does not match reliably).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible; the record carries both values.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D3. Which class a sweep record sweeps"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** \"The next round's reviewers receive the sweep records.\" Only\n  the claims of this round, or all of them?\n- **Answer:** All records of the open review scope. A boundary review's\n  packet carries `sweepRecords: {format: \"sweep-record/1\", guide, records}`:\n  the records built from this execution's `fixed` claims plus every record in\n  the inputs of earlier boundary reviews whose revision belongs to the same\n  open scope (`state.reviewScopes`). Each record carries `findingId`,\n  `classId`, `round` (the round that received the claim) and the sweep\n  fields. When a scope closes (integration, split or amendment) its records\n  stop being carried, like its round counter. The field is absent when there\n  are no records, so packets without claims are unchanged.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** only this round's claims (rejected: a round-5 reviewer\n  would not see the round-4 sweep it must hold the fix to); a new journal\n  event per record (rejected: more machinery; review inputs are already\n  retained and content-addressed).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D4. How sweep records reach the next round"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** How does \"the next round treats a missed site of a swept class\n  as a regression\" become mechanical?\n- **Answer:** When a boundary review is qualified, a finding whose `classId`\n  equals a record's `classId` (records of rounds up to the one being\n  qualified) and whose ID is not that record's claimed finding is a\n  regression of the previous fix, in addition to the existing path rule. So a\n  medium finding of a swept class keeps blocking in rounds 4-5. GateReeve\n  never sets a reviewer's `blocking` flag (correction policy): the reviewer\n  instructions and the packet guide tell reviewers that such a finding is a\n  regression and blocks in rounds 4-5. A finding raised before the sweep that\n  is still reported after it is also a missed site.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** compare the finding's origin round with the record's round\n  (rejected: a site already known when the sweep was claimed and still present\n  afterwards is exactly a missed site).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D5. A missed site of a swept class is a regression"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Fold in #134?\n- **Answer:** Yes. It is the same class as #144's cross-round regression\n  tracking and the same function (`findingObligations`' peer prepass). Two\n  changes: the prepass counts a peer's re-reported finding ID as still\n  present, so a peer's `resolved` cannot clear the origin; and reviews of one\n  round are grouped by the round policy's scope, round and revision (the\n  boundary review packet's `roundPolicy` gains `revision`), so two individual\n  selection-tagged reviews of one round are judged together. Inputs without a\n  revision keep the old grouping (`reviewRound`, else the result itself).\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** leave #134 for a separate PR (rejected: the fix sits in\n  the code this change edits and has the same tests).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D6. Fold in #134 (peer re-report loses a regression's origin)"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Planning reviews, light runs, metrics?\n- **Answer:** Planning reviews take no claims, so they are unchanged. Light\n  runs use the same review path and get the same behavior. Metrics for sweep\n  records are not added (AC11's remainder is #149).\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Reversibility:** additive later.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D7. Scope limits"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `approve-design` recorded after the Codex design review\n  (round 1) PASSed with no findings. Interview D1-D7 are agent answers for\n  Trent to check.\n- **Reversibility:** a later amendment can revise any entry.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S2. Design approved under autonomous-evaluator authority"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** All six low findings from the Claude spec review were taken:\n  F1-F4 tighten AC3, AC4, R2, R3 and R6; F6 adds plan step P7 (PR\n  description). F5 (design status line reads \"draft\") is left: editing\n  `design.md` would change the approved subject; the approval is recorded in\n  the protocol and in S2.\n- **Reversibility:** wording only.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S3. Spec review round 1 low findings addressed before implementation"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** Codex round 2 FAILed on two rubric-coverage gaps (R1 entry\n  points, R4 round 5). Both are fixed in the rubric; no behavior changed.\n- **Reversibility:** wording only.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S4. Spec review round 2 findings fixed"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** The #134 tests showed the two planned changes (prepass counts\n  re-reports, rounds grouped by scope, round and revision) were not enough.\n  When a peer re-reported a finding, `execution.js` replaced the finding's\n  history reference with the re-report, so the next round's history no longer\n  held the raising review and the origin was lost before qualification ran.\n  A re-report now keeps the first reference and records the re-report as\n  `reopened`, exactly as a `present` disposition already did. Small edit in\n  `execution.js` (shared with #142's work).\n- **Alternatives:** read origins from the whole run instead of the selected\n  history (rejected: a much wider change to history selection).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible; history may hold one more report.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S5. #134 needed a third change: keep the raising review in history"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `review.execute` checks claims before either reviewer starts,\n  in addition to the check in `gate.execute`. Without it, the per-reviewer\n  check turned an invalid claim into two `refused` outcomes recorded in a\n  `BOUNDARY_REVIEWED` event, which contradicts AC1 (\"record no result\").\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S6. `review.execute` refuses an invalid claim up front"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** AC4 as written: a finding is a class regression when its\n  `classId` matches a record whose `findingId` differs. When the implementer\n  claims two findings of one class fixed and one of them is still present, the\n  other record makes it a regression, so it keeps blocking in rounds 4-5. This\n  is the conservative reading: the sweep of the class claimed that site fixed\n  too. A single claimed finding still present is judged by its own origin.\n- **Alternatives:** exclude every claimed finding of the class (rejected for\n  now: it weakens the rule, and the spec states the narrower exclusion).\n- **Reversibility:** a one-line change in `sweptClassRegression`.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S7. Two fixed claims of one class: the other still present is a regression"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** The `--claims` option description now names `disposition` and\n  the sweep record.\n- **Reversibility:** text only.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S8. CLI help text for `--claims`"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** The first local Verification failed three checks because this\n  fresh worktree had no `cli/node_modules` (`Cannot find package\n  'commander'`). Installed with `npm ci --prefix cli` and reran the same\n  checks with unchanged arguments.\n- **Reversibility:** not applicable (environment).","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S9. Verification round 1 failed on a missing dependency install"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** Round 1 (head 850684d): both FAIL.\n  - Codex high `closed-scope-sweep-reuse`: records from a closed scope could\n    match a later scope with the same name and revision. Fixed with a scope\n    epoch (`roundPolicy.epoch`, the number of times the scope has closed),\n    required to match when collecting records and used in the #134 round key.\n  - Claude medium `CR-1`: the prepass counted a finding's first raise as a\n    re-report, so with same-revision grouping a re-review could never close\n    a finding its round raised; a later round then reopened it. Fixed: only a\n    re-report of an ID an earlier round raised counts as present.\n  - Codex medium / Claude low: no local desktop test receipt. Added\n    `npm test --prefix apps/desktop` to the local Verification.\n  - Codex low: no receipt for the `--claims` help text. Added a check.\n  - Claude low `CR-2` (a reused finding ID keeps the oldest raising report as\n    origin): not changed. It needs a reviewer to reuse an ID for a different\n    defect after its reports leave the packet; recorded for the PR.\n  The new tests fail on the round-1 code. Claims were passed in the old\n  `{findingId, claim}` shape because the run uses the installed rc.34 CLI.\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S10. Boundary review round 1 findings"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** Claims gain a required `disposition` (`fixed` or `disputed`);\n  `fixed` requires a sweep record, `disputed` forbids one. Callers that send\n  `{findingId, claim}` are refused naming `claims[i].disposition`.\n- **Alternatives:** an optional label (lets the sweep be skipped); a reviewer\n  disposition (reviewers verify, implementers claim).\n- **Reversibility:** input contract; more disposition values can be added.\n- **Source:** interview D1, D2.","file":"decisions.md","heading":"DEC-2. A fixed disposition is the implementer's claim, and it is required"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** A record takes `classId` from the claimed finding. Packets\n  carry the open scope's records, read from earlier boundary-review inputs;\n  a closed scope's records are not carried. A finding of a swept class other\n  than the record's own finding is a regression of the previous fix in\n  qualification; GateReeve never sets a reviewer's `blocking` flag. With two\n  fixed claims in one class, the other claimed finding still present counts as\n  a regression (S7).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.\n- **Source:** interview D3-D5, scratchpad S7.","file":"decisions.md","heading":"DEC-3. Swept class is the claimed finding's classId; records live in review inputs"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** Re-reported IDs count as present in the peer prepass; rounds\n  are grouped by the round policy's scope, round and revision; and a re-report\n  keeps the raising review in the next round's history (`execution.js`),\n  without which the origin was lost before qualification.\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.\n- **Source:** interview D6, scratchpad S5.","file":"decisions.md","heading":"DEC-4. #134 folded in, with a third change in execution.js"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `review.execute` refuses an invalid claim before either\n  reviewer starts (S6); the rounds 4-5 band instruction names missed sites of a\n  swept class; the `--claims` help text names the new fields (S8).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"decisions.md","heading":"DEC-5. Smaller implementation choices"}],"rule":"This is a goal run: the human was not asked. Assess every supplied agent-answered decision entry against the fixed mandate and the approved requirements. An entry that contradicts the mandate, exceeds its scope or makes an irreversible choice the human would need to make is blocking."},"candidate":{"bytes":1064580,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:5d4bfc25cf37d24f25eed614917503c839b6a805667a3757d8ff5c2d3738329d","preparationId":"144a15047baee019bddf6975620f0fa6a5ffeb5dd0497f606a5b75be815af005"},"claims":[{"claim":"Fixed (class: records or round groups of a closed review scope matching a later scope of the same name). roundPolicy now carries epoch = number of closed entries of the scope (round-policy.js scopeEpoch); scopeSweepRecords requires the input's epoch to match and the #134 round key includes it. Sites found by searching for roundPolicy.scope / reviewScopes consumers in sweep-records.js and review-findings.js: both fixed. Test: sweep-records.test.js R3 reopened-scope assertion.","findingId":"codex/review/closed-scope-sweep-reuse"},{"claim":"Fixed: the local Verification now runs npm test --prefix apps/desktop (185 tests, including stage-protocol and the projection manifest).","findingId":"codex/review/desktop-stage-receipt"},{"claim":"Fixed: a verification check asserts the --claims help text.","findingId":"codex/review/claims-help-receipt"},{"claim":"Fixed (class: a round's own reports treated as a peer's re-report). The prepass now counts a finding as present only when an earlier round group already raised its ID (review-findings.js raisedIn). Test: round-policy.test.js 'a same-revision re-review can close a finding its round raised', which fails on the round-1 code.","findingId":"claude-code/CR-1"},{"claim":"Fixed: same as the desktop receipt; npm test --prefix apps/desktop is now a local check.","findingId":"claude-code/CR-3"}],"configuration":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"claude-code","reasoningEffort":"high","reviewerSelection":"sha256:6894bdb313fa92f2ac9156eafa7c22b8690f37b36e998bb75371e0289f7b74e8"},"consumed":[{"bytes":7040,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:9de793c7512e6d783fcfaf0eac45312a78d8f7de64c715fdb8091f3ed517ce05"}],"contract":"gatereeve/current/8","coverageClaims":[],"environment":{"kind":"pinned-review-inputs"},"gates":["review"],"history":[{"bytes":10382,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:6905c4e2a031f188a38b10b05ebab28a4dab691a895de7001a88034fd8bc3f8e"},{"bytes":18850,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:71717d0382fe2dbf7061fdbb2bec6957df0499d3ec0b533fd3645b62e046c18d"}],"historyGaps":[],"integrations":null,"mandate":{"constraints":["Full rigor; the issue and docs/issues/tb-convergence-and-retirement/follow-up-feature.md (AC4) are the design input.","Where a choice is genuinely open, decide it on the merits, record it in the decision log and call it out in the PR description.","Verdict correction policy: GateReeve corrects only what follows from the verdict itself; meaning-changing gaps go to the repair turn.","Fold in issue #134 only if it falls naturally out of the change, and say so.","Stay 